Because our initial design has the smart glasses continuously process and record all frames,

221
00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:55.810
even when the staff are not interacting with any guests, this wasted a lot of power.

222
00:18:55.810 --> 00:19:04.910
So now the staff will activate the session by pressing the temple, and it will only stop after 15 seconds of audio silence.

223
00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:17.360
This allows for pauses during conversations, and it prevents the recording to be continuous.

224
00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:23.360
And this will cause a reduction in battery drainage by around 67%.

225
00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:30.860
Then our next iteration involves changing the processing from local to server.

226
00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:36.860
During our initial testing, we have all the heavy processing done within the glasses itself.

227
00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:40.860
And this causes the processing time to be around 1.6 seconds.

228
00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:48.860
Thus, we look into the pipeline and we discover that there are a few steps that have processing that is very heavy.

229
00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:54.860
So we move all of those steps into the server so that it can be processed faster.

230
00:19:54.860 --> 00:20:00.360
This causes a reduction of the processing time to around 1.1 in a second, which corresponds

231
00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:03.920
to around a 30% reduction in processing time.

232
00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:08.560
So for preference extraction, we have two approaches that we are diving deep into.

233
00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:13.500
So for the first approach, we have audio transcribed by Whisper, and we use the transcription

234
00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:17.840
to do a transcription that will be sent to a model for analysis.

235
00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:23.840
So we tested three models, and we found out that Qwen is the best model to extract preferences in

236
00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:34.450
the least amount of time. However, we found out that it might think questions or complaints

237
00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:40.370
as preferences, which is inaccurate. That is why we need further iterations in our next step,

238
00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:46.450
before incorporating video input, which is for approach to see whether the

239
00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:51.330
higher processing power is being justified by the video input.

240
00:20:51.330 --> 00:21:03.480
Then for the display of information, we are looking into the position of where the display

241
00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:04.980
information should be at.

242
00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:08.660
Then during our user testing, we found out that having the display information right

243
00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:17.680
inside the guest space is the best because this will reduce the time that the staff will

244
00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:21.680
look away from the guest and is more easily interpreted.

245
00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:28.100
To do so, we will have face detection within the glasses itself, which will figure out

246
00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:32.100
the coordinates of the face to display the information right beside the guest.

247
00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:40.540
However, if we have too much information about the guest, the long description may get too

248
00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:44.540
long and the staff have to read all these long descriptions while maintaining a conversation

249
00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:47.540
with the guest, which might be very disruptive.

250
00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:52.540
That is why we change these long descriptions into icons, so we split these main symbols

251
00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:55.540
into categories like food and room type.

252
00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:58.540
Then it will be labelled by markers.

253
00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:00.540
So a heart shape indicates a preference,

254
00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:03.540
and a warning sign indicates an allergy.

255
00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:09.690
This is so that the staff can easily interpret this design.

256
00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.690
Then for our proposed iteration,

257
00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:14.690
we found out that for returning customers,

258
00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:17.690
they might have a long list of preferences.

259
00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:20.690
So displaying them all at once will still be very disruptive.

260
00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:27.450
Hence, we propose contextual retrieval of preferences.

261
00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:32.510
So what the solution will do is that you will take in the audio transcription of the interaction

262
00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:38.090
and figure out the context of the interaction between guests.

263
00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:43.470
So for example, in a restaurant setting, when guests and staff are talking about food,

264
00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:47.800
they will show preferences only on food.

265
00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:54.060
And [unclear] the preferences on room type, which will disrupt the staff in the conversations. [Negation unclear in the audio.]

266
00:22:54.060 --> 00:23:00.310
So this is a simplified system architecture of our whole solution.

267
00:23:00.310 --> 00:23:06.310
The key points are the glasses and the server will be communicating through Wi-Fi, and the

268
00:23:06.310 --> 00:23:11.310
guest database will be stored in the server, so it can be actively updated by all staff.

269
00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:15.310
And at the same time, they can be retrieved by all staff, so that there is a preference

270
00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:18.880
continuity as stated in our design requirement.

271
00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:24.880
So we conducted concept walkthrough and product testing with 10 participants.

272
00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:31.270
However, they are not actual hotel staff, so it's mainly for usability testing.

273
00:23:31.270 --> 00:23:34.270
All of them mentioned that capturing information is easy.

274
00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:43.590
However, most participants found that to incorporate these visual cues into the interaction might be a bit challenging.

275
00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:48.270
So we have to conduct actual hotel testing

276
00:23:48.270 --> 00:23:51.510
to see whether is it cause of a lack of staff training

277
00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:56.160
or is there any other ways we can improve our process.

278
00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:59.500
So we weigh our smart glasses against the design requirement

279
00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:01.460
that [name unclear] had stated earlier.

280
00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:02.960
So for on-time service support,

281
00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:07.460
we have to achieve our less than one second service window.

282
00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:12.040
However, we only managed to reduce it to 1.18 seconds.

283
00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:14.620
However, we believe that this could be improved further,

284
00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.640
because now currently all the processing

285
00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:18.340
is being done on my laptop,

286
00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:21.060
which has limited processing power.

287
00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:22.620
Then for low-effort capture,

288
00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:25.140
I believe we can all agree that by simply

289
00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:28.300
tapping the temple of the glasses is very easy.

290
00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.800
Then for glanceable interaction, it is incomplete

291
00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:34.480
because it's still hard for staff

292
00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:37.720
to incorporate these visual cues into conversations,

293
00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:41.080
leading to occasional pauses and attention shifts.

294
00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:47.480
And for our preference continuity, the shared database really allows all staff to receive the most updated information.

295
00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:51.100
However, preferences are still not being extracted consistently.

296
00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:53.680
So we will look into it in the next semester.

297
00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:59.220
The main limitation of our smart glasses is the PDPA concerns.

298
00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:04.580
Because it requires data consent for collecting and retaining these preferences data.

299
00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:15.900
However, from our survey, most responders are willing to provide this data to have a more personalized service.

300
00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:23.020
This is our next step that we should be doing for our next semester.

301
00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:29.020
Key points such as preference extraction and contextual retrieval of preferences that I mentioned earlier.

302
00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:35.260
Then we will also conduct actual hotel staff usability pilot testing to identify is there a need

303
00:25:35.260 --> 00:25:40.020
for staff training to incorporate these visual cues into conversations naturally.

304
00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:49.020
We might also have cues for staff to ask guiding questions to capture more preferences data.

305
00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:55.020
Then lastly, we will implement LQA scoring for each staff to ensure that they adhere

306
00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:58.640
to service standards during their interaction with guests.

